WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.133
-That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.734
Welcome back. Tim Allen.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:04.868
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:06.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:08.534
-It's a pleasure
to have you back.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:10.767
Thank you for being here.
You look good, bud.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:12.968
-It's just great to be here.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:15.000
This is going --
It seems to have been 10 years.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.234
-It is, right?

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:17.501
I wanted to ask you, by the way,

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:19.200
I know you're good friends with
Jay Leno.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.400
And he just got out of
the hospital today.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.133
-He's great. They said -- He's
one of those heroes that --

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.601
Remember the "Deadpool" villain
that couldn't feel pain?

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:28.601
-Yeah.
-He's that guy. I saw him.

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:30.601
I called him the day of
the accident --

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:32.000
Our shops are close to
each other.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.634
We play around with
hot rods and stuff.

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:35.200
And one of his mechanics called
and said,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.300
"Jay's in a bad accident."

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.033
And I don't -- What do you do?

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.167
He's going to the hospital.
What am I'm going to do?

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:41.501
All I can do is leave a message.

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.300
He calls, like, back
five minutes later.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.534
"Hey, Tim, what's going on?"

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:45.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.801 --> 00:00:47.434
"Oh, sorry, Jay. I heard you
were in a bad accident."

00:00:47.434 --> 00:00:49.701
"Yeah, burned all over.
Third-degree burns on my hands,

00:00:49.701 --> 00:00:52.501
on my face, everything."
I go, "Uh, uh,

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:54.667
where -- What are you doing
right now?"

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.634
"I'm holding the phone.
It's really painful."

00:00:56.634 --> 00:00:58.267
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.267 --> 00:00:59.701
And he's not on painkillers.

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:01.834
He said, "And I'm not doing
painkillers."

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:04.601
"Why not?"
"Pain is there for a reason."

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:06.367
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my God.

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:07.968
-What do you say to that?

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:08.634
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, my God.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.467
-"O-Okay."

00:01:10.467 --> 00:01:13.367
"Okay. I think pain is there to
tell you to take a painkiller.

00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:14.400
Isn't that what
it's supposed to do?"

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.400
-That's what pain tells you.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.200
-Went and saw him and he's just
an amazing human being.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.400
He just wanted to get
out of the hospital.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.200
And he's --
It was one bit after another.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.234
We sat and talked for a lot.
-That's hilarious.

00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:27.934
-I get interviewed and they said
he's -- "He looks terrible."

00:01:27.934 --> 00:01:29.100
I said, "Well, he generally

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.300
didn't look that good
to begin with."

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.634
[ Laughter ]
-He would like that.

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:35.334
-Every comic said the same joke,
but when you get under pressure,

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.400
you say that, people go,
"Oh, that's terrible."

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.434
-Yeah, no. Jay would love that.
-Yeah.

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.334
But he's going -- He said he's
going with the Clooney look.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.901
And he looks really good
considering the grafts he's got.

00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:46.934
The picture today, it is amazing
what they can do.

00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:49.200
The hospital did a great job.
-We hope a speedy recovery, Jay.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.067
We love you, bud.
Yeah, it's good to hear.

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:52.534
Thank you for the update on him.
[ Applause ]

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:55.133
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.634
-It's probably good 'cause he
probably wants another comic

00:01:57.634 --> 00:01:58.734
to come in and do jokes,
which is good.

00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:00.501
-We were just riffing.
It was so stupid.

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.234
He's all bandaged up.

00:02:02.234 --> 00:02:03.501
"Yeah, I'm not on painkillers.

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:04.667
I don't feel like
no painkillers."

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:06.200
I kept looking.
I go, "Could I use some?"

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.400
Because I'm --
-Yeah.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.267
-"I'm in pain watching you."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:12.334
-You told me that you recently
went on a safari.

00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:15.968
-Oh, yeah. Because of COVID,
it was supposed to be --

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.534
whenever that was,
back in the '30s.

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:19.100
It seems like it was years ago.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.400
And we went to Zimbabwe,
Tanzania, Kenya,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.868
all through Eastern Africa.

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:28.567
And it was -- The country is
gigantic, number one.

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:30.100
-Yeah.
-And you get -- I've gone to

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.467
Egypt and Northern Africa,

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.334
which is no relation
to Southern Africa.

00:02:33.334 --> 00:02:34.701
I learned that.

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:38.501
But mostly, it's a huge zoo,
but it's the opposite.

00:02:38.501 --> 00:02:40.701
You're in a cage, and there's
animals running everywhere.

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:41.801
-Yeah, you're living in a zoo.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:43.133
-You're sitting in
this little vehicle.

00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:44.601
And I said,
"Do they know we're here?

00:02:44.601 --> 00:02:46.567
Do they seem to care
that you're there?"

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:50.100
So, the best memory I have,
driving up

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.801
in one of these vehicles,
and I looked, the guy says,

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:53.234
"If you look to your left,
there's a lion."

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:55.400
And you look -- and I couldn't
see it because he was

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.901
literally -- I could have
touched his nose.

00:02:56.901 --> 00:02:58.901
I went, "Oh, geez, oh, God."
-Wow.

00:02:58.901 --> 00:03:00.133
-And the lion's there.

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:01.334
And he goes, "His brother is
next to him."

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:02.467
I go, "I can see that.

00:03:02.467 --> 00:03:03.968
Are we supposed to be
this close?"

00:03:03.968 --> 00:03:05.434
He goes,
"They don't care about us."

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.501
-Is that right?
-"They care about the buffalo

00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:08.934
they killed on the other side of
the road."

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:10.434
You look over there
and there's a dead buffalo

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:12.300
with its butt eaten out,
whatever they -- lions --

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:15.000
It's kind of
a disgusting process.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.634
I guess they start...ass first.

00:03:17.634 --> 00:03:18.734
I've never seen anything
like it.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:19.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:21.534
Well, I don't know
what they were doing.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.534
But they weren't eating it.
We're going like this.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:25.234
They said they're just --
"What are they looking at?"

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:26.968
He goes, "Well, you --"
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:26.968 --> 00:03:30.834
"You never want to be between
a lion and their prey."

00:03:30.834 --> 00:03:33.033
And I'm going, "So you wouldn't

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.033
want to be kind of
where we are?"

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:37.300
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly where we are right now.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.234
-And the driver goes, "Oh, man.
We should back up."

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.934
-Yeah, hold on a second.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.934 --> 00:03:43.634
"Is this the first time
you've done this, sir?"

00:03:43.634 --> 00:03:46.200
-I want to mention, by the way,
your stand-up tour.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.167
You're going to be in North
Charleston, South Carolina.

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:50.834
And on January 7th,
you're in Boston.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:52.400
-Good one.
-Thank you.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.934
You can check all this out
on timallen.com.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.767
-This is why we rehearse.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.767 --> 00:03:58.167
North Charleston.

00:03:58.167 --> 00:03:59.634
Have you ever been there before?

00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:02.467
-I've been both of these places.
This has been a year.

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:05.234
Speaking of, you know, Leno goes
out like 200 dates a year.

00:04:05.234 --> 00:04:08.033
I've been doing comedy for
30 years, the same opening act.

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:10.467
My whole business got
started on --

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:12.267
My first act was
"Home Improvement."

00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:14.334
I sold that. I've done this.

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:17.434
And we were talking backstage
about this, about --

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:20.033
Comedy has changed a little bit.

00:04:20.033 --> 00:04:23.300
Comics don't have, um, unions.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.501
We're a burlesque union is
really what it is.

00:04:25.501 --> 00:04:26.968
So people can steal your jokes.

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:28.534
That's just what they do.
-Yeah.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:31.067
-And one -- And we're all
looking for a hook.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.534
So, I'm doing this gig in Canada
when I first started out.

00:04:33.534 --> 00:04:36.267
And NBC wanted to see me at the
time because I was getting

00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:38.701
this hook with this grunting
and all this tool stuff.

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:41.701
And I'm following a guy named
Chris Lyman.

00:04:41.701 --> 00:04:44.968
And you look out the -- and his
manager is introducing him

00:04:44.968 --> 00:04:48.167
buck naked with the ugliest male
body parts

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:50.100
I've ever seen in my life
from the back.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.767
They're not that handsome
on any angle.

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:54.234
But from -- on a stool, you're
going, "Boy, if you can see

00:04:54.234 --> 00:04:56.167
the unit on a stool --"
You're going,

00:04:56.167 --> 00:04:57.634
"What the hell is
going on here?"

00:04:57.634 --> 00:05:00.133
-Yeah.
-The guy goes out buck naked,

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.567
does his whole act and ends --

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:05.167
I don't know how he was holding
it or where it was exactly

00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:08.033
but he had a Roman candle
sticking out of his rear end,

00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:09.567
and he's spinning around going,

00:05:09.567 --> 00:05:11.567
"There's no business
like show business."

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:13.100
And he's spinning around

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.501
and there's a Roman candle
going in the audience.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:19.534
And the guy -- It goes, "Fft,
fft, fffft," and it goes out.

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:22.100
He goes back, and the guy says,
"Thank you, Chris.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.834
And now comedian Tim Allen."

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:25.968
[ Laughter ]
-"How do I follow that?"

00:05:25.968 --> 00:05:30.300
-How do you follow a guy with
a Roman candle in his rear end?

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.367
He comes backstage on his way
back, covered up and go,

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:34.901
"Excuse me, excuse me,
excuse me."

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:36.667
Like, "Oh, so, now you're modest
after this."

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.701
[ Laughter ]
-Now you're professional. Yeah.

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:40.801
-The whole room is fill with
half smoke --

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:43.467
acrid smoke with NBC execs.

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.567
Everybody is out there going --
[ Coughing ]

00:05:45.567 --> 00:05:47.834
-You go, "What the --
-"What did I just see?"

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:49.334
-Yeah. But yet, you did it.

00:05:49.334 --> 00:05:50.901
-Yeah, I did the bit.
-You followed it.

00:05:50.901 --> 00:05:53.534
-Followed the bit. And my
career, well, it took off.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:54.601
So I guess it was something
for somebody.

00:05:54.601 --> 00:05:56.501
-You did pretty well. Yeah.

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:59.801
I want to talk about your new
series, "The Santa Clauses."

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:01.667
You're getting back in the suit.

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:04.200
Does the holiday --
Is it a change for you now?

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.234
-At this time of year,
it's become kind of

00:06:06.234 --> 00:06:08.100
this iconic piece come back.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.200
And the only reason
I wanted to do it,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.501
I'm kind of a sci-fi freak,
and I want to make sure

00:06:12.501 --> 00:06:15.067
all the holes in the script
are -- I never understood,

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:17.300
even in the movie,
why the elves, when I showed up

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.400
at the North Pole, didn't go,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.300
"Hey, what happened
to the other guy?"

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.934
You know, I just hop
in the sleigh

00:06:21.934 --> 00:06:23.367
with the kid and we go off.

00:06:23.367 --> 00:06:24.834
No one ever asks, "Where's --

00:06:24.834 --> 00:06:27.534
Does Mrs. Claus know
that the other guy is dead?"

00:06:27.534 --> 00:06:29.434
"Did the other guy die?"
-There's holes in the plot.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:31.868
-It was huge holes. "Why does
Mrs. Claus have a name?

00:06:31.868 --> 00:06:34.200
What's her first name?"
And that -- So, they --

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.901
We came up with this script,
"The Clauses."

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:37.801
I have a family now.

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:39.701
And they end up doing all --

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.400
They fill up the holes
in all this.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.100
I should interrupt this.
My kid was in it.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.734
And my youngest kid, I wanted
her to be an elf as a surprise.

00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:50.434
She ended up reading
the dialogue so well

00:06:50.434 --> 00:06:52.501
that the kid got the lead in it.

00:06:52.501 --> 00:06:53.968
-Yeah, she's --
-She's Santa's daughter.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:56.200
-She's gorgeous, by the way.
I met her backstage, Elizabeth.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.601
-It's just -- It was the most
amazing occurrence.

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:02.067
She'll even say it. I get --
No matter how mad I am,

00:07:02.067 --> 00:07:04.701
I come on the set, big set they
got in there in Hollywood.

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:06.434
And they go, "He's on the move,
he's on the move.

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:09.501
Santa's on the move."
And I'm just mad and sweaty.

00:07:09.501 --> 00:07:11.601
I'm already mad.
But you show up on set.

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:14.167
You got adults, grips,
camera people, sound.

00:07:14.167 --> 00:07:16.167
Everybody goes, "Oh, look."

00:07:16.167 --> 00:07:17.200
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.934
-Because you've become
this thing.

00:07:18.934 --> 00:07:21.667
-Jolly.
-And so I got to stop swearing.

00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:25.267
-Yeah, yeah. But I -- It's
an interesting story about

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:27.234
your daughter getting cast
because she's --

00:07:27.234 --> 00:07:28.267
How old is she?

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:29.968
-She was 13 the day she started.

00:07:29.968 --> 00:07:33.267
The girl in the movie, Sandra --
Sandra Claus is 13.

00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:37.100
And, again, it was one of these
audition processes that I just

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.834
wanted her to read, say,
"Hi, Santa,"

00:07:38.834 --> 00:07:40.634
as a Christmas gift as an elf.
Just walk by once.

00:07:40.634 --> 00:07:41.734
-And you thought it would be
something fun

00:07:41.734 --> 00:07:43.367
for you and your wife
to always have.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:44.734
-She's great in school.

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:45.934
My daughter is real good
in sports.

00:07:45.934 --> 00:07:47.767
We just didn't want her
getting this.

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.901
-Yeah.
-She read it so well,

00:07:50.901 --> 00:07:52.200
drilled it.
-She nailed it.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.968
-Oh, God.
-Yeah, I have a picture of you

00:07:53.968 --> 00:07:56.067
and your daughter here.
It's so cute.

00:07:56.067 --> 00:07:57.501
[ Audience aws ]

00:07:57.501 --> 00:07:59.501
And you were telling me that you
go -- you got real emotional.

00:07:59.501 --> 00:08:01.567
-Oh, well, this is
a real emotional scene.

00:08:01.567 --> 00:08:06.434
This one comes out --
The first two, as they dropped,

00:08:06.434 --> 00:08:08.934
and now the series starts
this week.

00:08:08.934 --> 00:08:12.100
And this scene, I'm trying to
convince her I want to retire

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.234
and go back to Chicago where I
started the whole thing.

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:16.934
And she says, "I'm afraid."
And I said -- I get choked up.

00:08:16.934 --> 00:08:18.701
She goes, "I'm afraid.
Are you afraid?

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:20.167
Have you ever been afraid, Dad?"

00:08:20.167 --> 00:08:22.267
I said, "of course,
but I would be less afraid

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:24.133
if we could be afraid together."

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:26.567
And I'm doing that with my kid.
I'm looking in her eyes.

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:27.734
I'm going, "Whoa, man."

00:08:27.734 --> 00:08:29.634
This was -- It was --
It was unbelievable.

00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:31.567
-Everyone was crying.
-Oh, everybody was crying.

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:33.000
-It's awesome.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.067
It's heartwarming
but it's also funny.

00:08:35.067 --> 00:08:37.133
I tell you who really is funny.

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:38.734
Peyton Manning gets
some good laughs.

00:08:38.734 --> 00:08:40.100
And Peyton --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.467
Here's you and Peyton Manning.

00:08:41.467 --> 00:08:43.334
How is it like doing a scene
with Peyton Manning?

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:45.901
-Well, this was nuts.
I'm a huge football fan.

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:48.901
-Yeah.
-Sorry about the Giants.

00:08:48.901 --> 00:08:50.467
Well, I'm from Detroit,

00:08:50.467 --> 00:08:53.267
so it was the weirdest football
game ever.

00:08:53.267 --> 00:08:55.133
-Yeah, I know. But they're still
having a good season.

00:08:55.133 --> 00:08:56.667
People are still happy.
-I understand.

00:08:56.667 --> 00:08:58.100
I'm a Giants fan from way back.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.601
Anyway, Y.A. Tittle sent me
a letter when I was a kid.

00:09:00.601 --> 00:09:03.033
He's a big Giants --
Long story short.

00:09:03.033 --> 00:09:07.667
Anyhow, Peyton's on the show,
but I'm dressed as Santa Claus.

00:09:07.667 --> 00:09:09.200
So he's doing the same look
to me.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:10.667
He's going, "Ho, ho.
Look at you."

00:09:10.667 --> 00:09:12.667
I go, "No, man. I'm a fan."

00:09:12.667 --> 00:09:14.968
But I'm under seven feet of
makeup."

00:09:14.968 --> 00:09:17.834
And I was like a kid
because he's really good.

00:09:17.834 --> 00:09:20.601
And he said, "Oh, look at you.
I want a football."

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:22.000
He's doing -- He's like --

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.601
-He never said,
"I want a football."

00:09:23.601 --> 00:09:24.968
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.968 --> 00:09:26.934
-He was so natural and so funny.

00:09:26.934 --> 00:09:28.734
He's so engaging because I --

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.901
The story is I'm auditioning
new guys to be Santa.

00:09:31.901 --> 00:09:34.100
And he's got
a good football record.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.634
And I thought maybe that would
make a good Santa Claus.

00:09:36.634 --> 00:09:38.300
Drilled the scene.
Drilled the scene.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.434
-I want to show a clip.

00:09:39.434 --> 00:09:41.367
Here's Tim Allen
in "The Santa Clauses."

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:42.801
Take a look at this.

00:09:42.801 --> 00:09:44.234
-Okay, okay. How about this?

00:09:44.234 --> 00:09:46.734
Give us your best "ho, ho, ho."

00:09:46.734 --> 00:09:48.634
-Oh, okay. Here we go.

00:09:48.634 --> 00:09:51.634
Ho, ho, homaha! Homaha!

00:09:51.634 --> 00:09:54.367
You see that?
I called a little audible there.

00:09:54.367 --> 00:09:58.033
-He --
-He's odd, yet somehow perfect.

00:09:58.033 --> 00:09:59.367
-He's odd, alright.

00:09:59.367 --> 00:10:01.234
I don't know if we have a hat
big enough for that head.

00:10:01.234 --> 00:10:03.434
Phew.
Look at the size of that thing.

00:10:03.434 --> 00:10:05.868
-What's gotten into you?
We need to find someone quickly.

00:10:05.868 --> 00:10:07.334
-What's the rush?

00:10:07.334 --> 00:10:09.501
-Well, breaking in new Santas
take time.

00:10:09.501 --> 00:10:10.834
-Breaking in a Santa.

00:10:10.834 --> 00:10:12.334
We're talking about Santas,
not horses.

00:10:12.334 --> 00:10:14.033
-I know. Horses listen.

00:10:14.033 --> 00:10:15.734
-Ha, that's a good one.

00:10:15.734 --> 00:10:17.033
-Are you still here?

00:10:17.033 --> 00:10:19.667
-Yeah. Tim Allen, everyone!

00:10:19.667 --> 00:10:21.834
New episodes of
"The Santa Clauses"

00:10:21.834 --> 00:10:25.100
are available
every Wednesday on Disney+.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:27.067
Lacey Chabert joins us
after the break.

00:10:27.067 --> 00:10:28.467
Stick around, everybody.

